13:41:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair point - you're right, and I probably over-explained something you'd already directly observed. The dive-bombing and chasing IS the dominance display, full stop. That's what people watching feeders see constantly. I threw in that bit about "actual contact occurring in more intense disputes" almost as a hedge, but that's not really the everyday reality at a feeder. Real physical contact is rare - the whole point of these behaviors is that the chasing and diving substitutes for fighting. A bird backs off because it's outmatched in the chase/display, not because it got hit. So yeah - dive-bomb, chase, repeat. That's basically the whole game for feeder dominance. The other stuff I mentioned (gorget flashing, chirping, etc.) does happen too, but the chase is the main event you're seeing, and it works precisely because it doesn't need to escalate to contact for one bird to back down.
